            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "We first reduce to the case where P has all of its roots in [0,1]. If this is not the case, we can factor  $ P(x) $ as  $ Q(x)R(x) $, where Q has all roots in the interval and R has none. Then R is either always positive or always negative on [0,1]; assume the former. Let k be the largest positive real number such that  $ R(x) - kx \\geq 0 $ on [0,1]; then",
          "block_bbox": [
            315,
            2448,
            1251,
            2781
          ],
          "block_id": 15,
          "block_order": 15,
          "group_id": 15,
          "block_polygon_points": [
