        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Thus it suffices to prove that if  $ Q(x) $ is a monic polynomial of degree 1999 with all of its roots in  $ [0,1] $, then  $ \\int_{0}^{1}|Q(x)|dx\\geq D $ for some constant D>0. But the integral of  $ \\int_{0}^{1}\\prod_{i=1}^{1999}|x-r_{i}|dx $ is a continuous function for  $ r_{i}\\in[0,1] $. The product of all of these intervals is compact, so the integral achieves a minimum value for some  $ r_{i} $. This minimum is the desired D.",
          "block_bbox": [
            1410,
            1095,
            2351,
            1451
          ],
